{
  "applinks": {
    "details": [
      {
        "appIDs": [
          "PLACEHOLDER_REPLACE_WITH_APPLE_TEAM_ID.com.vallospaces.app"
        ],
        "components": [
          {
            "/": "/auth/callback*",
            "comment": "THE AUTH RETURN, AND IT MUST BE HANDED TO THE APP. Social sign-in is off on the native shell (STORE-02/03: Apple signs in through the native sheet), but the email confirmation and password-reset links still land here, and a sign-up started in the app set its PKCE verifier cookie in the WEB VIEW's jar, not Safari's. If Safari loads this URL, the exchange runs in a jar with no verifier and the person is returned to an app that is still signed out. src/lib/native/deep-links.ts hands it to the web view. Ordered FIRST, because an AASA components array is read in order and the exclusion below would otherwise swallow it."
          },
          {
            "/": "/auth/*",
            "exclude": true,
            "comment": "Everything else under /auth stays out. Only the callback is a return journey; a sign-in page, a password reset form and an email confirmation are all pages a person should meet wherever they opened them."
          },
          {
            "/": "/api/*",
            "exclude": true,
            "comment": "Excluded. These are route handlers, including the Paystack webhook. Nothing here is a page a person should ever land on."
          },
          {
            "/": "/checkout/*",
            "exclude": true,
            "comment": "Excluded. This is where Paystack returns a guest after a hosted card payment, and that return has to complete in the browser session that started it."
          },
          {
            "/": "/wallet*",
            "exclude": true,
            "comment": "Excluded for the same reason as checkout: it is the return path for wallet funding."
          },
          {
            "/": "/trips*",
            "exclude": true,
            "comment": "Excluded although it is a Stays path. /trips is a signed-in own-data surface with nothing on it for anybody else, so a forwarded link can only ever land its reader on a sign-in wall inside the app, which is a worse first meeting than a browser tab. The same reasoning keeps /bookings, /home and /search off this list."
          },
          {
            "/": "/listing/*",
            "comment": "The case that pays for this whole feature. A listing forwarded on WhatsApp opens in the app for anyone who has it installed."
          },
          {
            "/": "/around/*",
            "comment": "An area page. Shared the same way a listing is, and the product prints this address in the propose-an-area form."
          },
          {
            "/": "/u/*",
            "comment": "A member profile. The product tells people their address is vallospaces.com/u/handle, so it has to open here."
          },
          {
            "/": "/post/*",
            "comment": "A single post, shared out of the social layer."
          },
          {
            "/": "/stories/*",
            "comment": "A single story, shared out of the social layer."
          },
          {
            "/": "/stay/*",
            "comment": "One stay. The Stays side's twin of /listing/, and the reason this block exists: lib/side.constants.ts forces the Stays shell for this path, and it never got the chance to run because the system handed the URL to a browser instead of the app."
          },
          {
            "/": "/stays/*",
            "comment": "Stays search and the shelves beneath it. A filtered search shared in a chat is how a group decides where to stay."
          },
          {
            "/": "/restaurant/*",
            "comment": "One restaurant. Shared exactly as a stay is, and it opens the Stays shell for the same reason."
          },
          {
            "/": "/restaurants/*",
            "comment": "The restaurant shelves and their search."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
